README.OSK -- what was changed to build CMDS/cuts herecuts_encode.c: the date in the identifier packet is now written with`t->tm_year % 100'.  The format is YYMMDD and the decoder reads it backwith `%6s'; from 2000 on tm_year has three digits (126 for 2026), so theencoder wrote `1260926', the decoder took six of those seven characters,and everything after the date -- the file type and the file NAME -- waslost.  `cuts -d' then answered "'' Encoded as '   '" and saved thedata as `.0'.  One line; the original is cuts_encode.c.ori.CMDS/cuts is built from this tree with the collection's driver (`-qm',trap-free), 2026-09-26.  The archive's build linked the cio trap handlerand was caught by the putc/getc selector mismatch (DOC/README-CIO):`-e' wrote `!0!0!0' lines and then asked for memory until os9exec's 68karena was full, and `-d' kept only the line ends.  Measured both ways intools/datatests/PA.cases: the new build encodes /dd/SYS/motd and decodesit back to an identical file.The *.r files beside the sources are objects left by an earlier build;they are not used.